          "email": "adilger@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-20 14:48:02.000000000",
          "tz": -360
        },
        "subject": "LU-20690 lod: write 1+N EC data around lost OST mirror",
        "message": "LU-20690 lod: write 1+N EC data around lost OST mirror\n\nA write to an EC file would fail while the OST of a data stripe in a\ncomponent of 1-data raid sets (--ec 1+N, or one data stripe) was\nunavailable: the data mirror is the only mirror that can be the write\nprimary, and its object could not be written.  Yet each of the N\nparity objects of such a raid set holds a copy of the data object at\nthe same object offsets.\n\nWhen a write era starts (RDONLY -> WRITE_PENDING) the parity is still\nin sync, so lod_ec_swap_unavail_data() swaps each data object of such\na component in the write extent that is on a disconnected or\ndeactivated OST with a parity object of the same raid set on an\navailable OST.  An OST that is only full or takes no new objects is\nstill written.  The data mirror stays a complete write primary, and\nthe parity component, which the write stales as before, holds the\nunavailable object until the next resync rewrites it.  The swapped-in\nobject may be outside the pool or OST list of the data component.\n\nThe swapped-in object counts for the file size, so it is only used if\nits size is that of the data object for the file size that the last\nresync stored as strict SOM.  A copy written by resync with O_DIRECT\nended on a page boundary past a file size that is not page aligned,\nand at the last data when the file had a trailing hole, so the EC\nresync now truncates the parity mirror to the file size when EOF is\ninside a component of 1-data raid sets, as FLR resync does for a\nmirror, and only warns if that fails.  Parity resynced by an older\nlfs, left past EOF when EOF is in a component with larger raid sets,\nor of an EC layout version that the MDS does not know, is not swapped\nin.\n\nAfter the layout change, the MDD rewrites the filter_fid of the\nswapped-in object with its new stripe index and component in a\ntransaction of its own, as after a layout swap, so that it names the\nstripe it now is for LFSCK and lfsck_verify_pfid.  The object swapped\nout to the parity component is on the unavailable OST and keeps its\nold filter_fid.  OSP OST objects can now be invalidated, so that the\nMDS fetches the size of a parity copy from the OST.\n\nSwapping only happens at the start of a write era, while the parity\ncomponent is in sync, for the components of the extent that the\nfirst write of the era touches.  An OST lost during a write era, and\na 1-data raid set in a component with larger raid sets, whose parity\nis not at the data offsets, still need the OST to return.\n\nAdd sanity-ec test_313 to write 1+2 files with a data OST unavailable,\nfor an unaligned size, a sparse tail, a second PFL component and the\nsecond stripe of a 2-stripe component, and check the swap, the file\nsize and the data before and after resync.  Add test_314 to check that\nstale parity past EOF is not swapped in, and test_315 to swap the\nsecond stripe of a 2-stripe 1+2 file twice.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial testlist=sanity-ec,sanity-flr ostcount=8\nSigned-off-by: Andreas Dilger <adilger@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5 ltvm llm_code_and_review_tools\nChange-Id: Iab02681175f14e69d78441647d6f1bd3b53ebbe5\n"
      },
